00:04:45.238 --> 00:04:46.338
- So--

00:04:46.338 --> 00:04:47.538
- [Neil] The
holographic universe.

00:04:47.538 --> 00:04:49.008
- So a hologram, the
whole idea of a hologram

00:04:49.008 --> 00:04:50.778
is that you really have
two-dimensional information

00:04:50.778 --> 00:04:53.148
but it creates the illusion
of being three-dimensional.

00:04:53.148 --> 00:04:54.948
That's just what
a hologram means.

00:04:54.948 --> 00:04:56.148
- Yeah 'cause when
you look at it,

00:04:56.148 --> 00:04:58.518
like your credit card
might have a holographic

00:04:58.518 --> 00:04:59.458
bird on it--

00:04:59.458 --> 00:05:00.918
- [Janna] Right, but it's

00:05:00.918 --> 00:05:02.288
a two dimensional object.
- My binder in middle school.

00:05:02.288 --> 00:05:03.788
- It works in two dimensions
because it's flat.

00:05:03.788 --> 00:05:05.558
- It's flat, but it looks like
a full three-volumed thing,

00:05:05.558 --> 00:05:08.828
okay, so the reason why
these physicists coined it

00:05:08.828 --> 00:05:12.538
holographic is precisely
because they're imagining

00:05:12.538 --> 00:05:15.468
that all of the information
that is required

00:05:15.468 --> 00:05:17.368
to understand the
universe can be encoded

00:05:17.368 --> 00:05:21.278
on just a surface as opposed
to in the full space.

00:05:21.278 --> 00:05:22.748
So for a black hole this arose

00:05:22.748 --> 00:05:24.508
because if you
look, a black hole

00:05:24.508 --> 00:05:27.518
doesn't physically have
anything at its surface,

00:05:27.518 --> 00:05:29.388
but we imagine the event
horizon as a surface,

00:05:29.388 --> 00:05:32.918
the region beyond which
no information can escape.

00:05:32.918 --> 00:05:36.458
And the idea was that
everything about the black hole

00:05:36.458 --> 00:05:38.058
that all the
information a black hole

00:05:38.058 --> 00:05:41.968
could possibly contain is
equivalent to the amount

00:05:41.968 --> 00:05:44.598
of information that can
be packed on the surface

00:05:44.598 --> 00:05:46.138
and nothing more

00:05:46.138 --> 00:05:49.138
and you cannot, and they've
proven this over many years,

00:05:49.138 --> 00:05:52.738
back since Hawking, you
cannot have more information

00:05:52.738 --> 00:05:54.808
than the information you
can pack on the surface.

00:05:54.808 --> 00:05:56.478
And the reason
why this becomes--

00:05:56.478 --> 00:05:57.708
- You're saying

00:05:57.708 --> 00:05:59.178
more information can't
get into the black hole

00:05:59.178 --> 00:06:01.648
than can be stored on the
surface of the event horizon.

00:06:01.648 --> 00:06:03.748
- Yes, that you can prove
that the information

00:06:03.748 --> 00:06:06.388
content of the black hole
scales like the surface area

00:06:06.388 --> 00:06:07.618
of the black hole,
the event horizon,

00:06:07.618 --> 00:06:08.958
and not like the volume.

00:06:08.958 --> 00:06:11.658
And so the idea would be,

00:06:11.658 --> 00:06:13.558
well we could maybe
make an object--

00:06:13.558 --> 00:06:14.868
- He shook his head yeah,

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:16.068
get that calculation.

00:06:16.068 --> 00:06:16.898
- I used to have those
hologram stickers,

00:06:16.898 --> 00:06:19.268
of like, cats,

00:06:19.268 --> 00:06:22.238
does that get me anywhere
close to understanding?

00:06:22.238 --> 00:06:25.308
- So you know it's one thing
to kind of think about that

00:06:25.308 --> 00:06:27.208
but then it took really
years before people

00:06:27.208 --> 00:06:28.978
like Leonard Susskind
started saying,

00:06:28.978 --> 00:06:29.908
"Oh wait--"

00:06:29.908 --> 00:06:31.008
- Is he a physicist at Stanford?

00:06:31.008 --> 00:06:32.218
- He's a physicist at Stanford

00:06:32.218 --> 00:06:33.878
and just an incredibly
creative thinker.

00:06:33.878 --> 00:06:36.018
- [Neil] Larry Susskind.

00:06:36.018 --> 00:06:37.248
- Yeah and great New
Yorker from the Bronx.

00:06:37.248 --> 00:06:39.988
He was a--
- Bronx in the house

00:06:39.988 --> 00:06:40.988
- Yeah.

00:06:40.988 --> 00:06:41.988
- BX.

00:06:41.988 --> 00:06:43.028
- Bronx in the hologram.

00:06:43.028 --> 00:06:44.088
- I love Lenny's accent.

00:06:44.088 --> 00:06:44.858
- We brought him a hat up there

00:06:46.158 --> 00:06:47.358
I don't know if it
shows up in the thing.

00:06:47.358 --> 00:06:48.628
- He's got that tough
guy accent still.

00:06:48.628 --> 00:06:50.798
So he really started
to think about this

00:06:50.798 --> 00:06:53.438
like let's say I try
to have information

00:06:53.438 --> 00:06:54.968
that actually scales
like the volume

00:06:54.968 --> 00:06:56.708
like so that would be
a lot more information

00:06:56.708 --> 00:06:58.808
than you would think you
could pack on the surface

00:06:58.808 --> 00:07:00.478
and you can prove that,
well then you'd just make

00:07:00.478 --> 00:07:01.908
a black hole, and we
know about black holes

00:07:01.908 --> 00:07:05.448
fundamentally that you
cannot have more information

00:07:05.448 --> 00:07:07.778
in the black hole than you
can put on the surface.

00:07:07.778 --> 00:07:10.118
- But what does it mean to
put information on a surface

00:07:10.118 --> 00:07:11.418
that does not exist?

00:07:11.418 --> 00:07:14.258
- That's very subtle,
so it's really the idea,

00:07:14.258 --> 00:07:15.788
there's two ways of saying it.

00:07:15.788 --> 00:07:17.728
One is to just say, oh,
the information content

00:07:17.728 --> 00:07:20.798
of the black hole has been
proven to scale like the area

00:07:20.798 --> 00:07:22.898
and then the string
theorists started to think

00:07:22.898 --> 00:07:25.438
well maybe that physically
means it's like there

00:07:25.438 --> 00:07:26.868
on the horizon,

00:07:26.868 --> 00:07:28.668
- [Neil] The event horizon.

00:07:28.668 --> 00:07:30.138
- The event horizon, that
as something falls into

00:07:30.138 --> 00:07:33.238
the black hole, it might
appear to the poor astronaut

00:07:33.238 --> 00:07:35.808
that thinks they're
falling into the black hole

00:07:35.808 --> 00:07:37.548
that they have crossed
the event horizon

00:07:37.548 --> 00:07:39.318
and met their fate
in the interior

00:07:39.318 --> 00:07:41.818
but to us on the
outside it may appear,

00:07:41.818 --> 00:07:43.218
and this is a string
theory suggestion,

00:07:43.218 --> 00:07:46.618
that it's actually physically
smeared on the outside,

00:07:46.618 --> 00:07:50.358
in the quantum string
states that are permitted

00:07:50.358 --> 00:07:52.958
on the outside, and that
it never gets inside.

00:07:52.958 --> 00:07:53.898
- So what about the
fact that if I watched

00:07:53.898 --> 00:07:54.828
someone fall into a black hole,

00:07:54.828 --> 00:07:57.298
I see their time slow down?

00:07:57.298 --> 00:08:00.098
And at the event
horizon it has stopped.

00:08:00.098 --> 00:08:01.568
- [Janna] Exactly.

00:08:01.568 --> 00:08:03.308
- So can we say that all
this stops at the black hole

00:08:03.308 --> 00:08:04.738
as far as I can see?

00:08:04.738 --> 00:08:06.778
- I mean it certainly is
driven by some of those early

00:08:06.778 --> 00:08:09.578
confusion about that.

00:08:09.578 --> 00:08:12.578
So it does seem, as far as we're
concerned it never crosses.

00:08:12.578 --> 00:08:16.688
But to the astronaut themselves,

00:08:16.688 --> 00:08:18.258
- They just fall straight--
- they sail right through.

00:08:18.258 --> 00:08:22.858
So it's literally suggesting,
like quantum complementarity

00:08:22.858 --> 00:08:24.588
created this whole,
there are two things

00:08:24.588 --> 00:08:26.998
that seem incompatible that
can exist at the same time.

00:08:26.998 --> 00:08:29.398
It's literally suggesting that
the astronaut both falls in

00:08:29.398 --> 00:08:30.498
and does not.

00:08:30.498 --> 00:08:31.938
- And is smeared on the surface.

00:08:31.938 --> 00:08:33.498
- And is smeared on the
surface, but there's no single

00:08:33.498 --> 00:08:35.968
person in the universe
who would be able

00:08:35.968 --> 00:08:39.308
to experience both things,
so no single person

00:08:39.308 --> 00:08:40.578
has a contradiction.

00:08:40.578 --> 00:08:43.178
Woo, I say it's--

00:08:43.178 --> 00:08:44.678
- When you talk about encoding
information on the surface

00:08:44.678 --> 00:08:46.648
do you mean like the way like
there's a California tattoo

00:08:46.648 --> 00:08:47.778
on Adam Levine?

00:08:47.778 --> 00:08:49.048
(Janna and Neil laughing)

00:08:49.048 --> 00:08:50.348
Where we know he's
from, California?

00:08:50.348 --> 00:08:52.018
- He's only tattoos on
the surface, it just--

00:08:52.018 --> 00:08:54.158
- That's all he is,
there's nothing inside.

00:08:54.158 --> 00:08:56.288
- It looks like a
three-dimensional body

00:08:56.288 --> 00:08:58.258
but it's really
just skin tattoos.

00:08:58.258 --> 00:08:59.828
- Okay.

00:08:59.828 --> 00:09:01.598
- So tell me something,
if I think people

00:09:01.598 --> 00:09:04.728
freeze at the surface,
then they're not living out

00:09:04.728 --> 00:09:07.098
their lives, we're
living out our lives,

00:09:07.098 --> 00:09:08.138
how could we be in the,

00:09:08.138 --> 00:09:09.638
every hologram I've seen,

00:09:09.638 --> 00:09:12.308
it's a frozen image, it's not,

00:09:12.308 --> 00:09:14.638
or it repeats in
some trivial way.

00:09:14.638 --> 00:09:15.678
- So that's what we would see,

00:09:15.678 --> 00:09:16.948
we would see them freeze.

00:09:16.948 --> 00:09:19.618
- But I'm not frozen, so
how can I be a hologram?

00:09:19.618 --> 00:09:21.918
- So oh, I see what
you're talking,

00:09:21.918 --> 00:09:23.118
so it would be--

00:09:23.118 --> 00:09:25.118
- Wait, wait, I don't
think I'm frozen.

00:09:25.118 --> 00:09:26.518
(Janna and Harrison laughing)

00:09:26.518 --> 00:09:27.318
- So you--

00:09:28.758 --> 00:09:30.228
- It's like the end of the Mary
Tyler Moore opening credits

00:09:30.228 --> 00:09:31.758
where she like
freezes with the hat?

00:09:31.758 --> 00:09:32.998
- Oh she freezes there
when she flips up her hat.

00:09:32.998 --> 00:09:34.258
- Right, is that what
we're talking about?

00:09:34.258 --> 00:09:34.928
- Actually, how old
are you? (laughing)

00:09:34.928 --> 00:09:36.798
- I have no age.

00:09:36.798 --> 00:09:38.868
- You're just a fan
of Mary Tyler Moore.

00:09:38.868 --> 00:09:40.138
- Yeah.

00:09:40.138 --> 00:09:41.568
- She was off the air since

00:09:41.568 --> 00:09:42.738
- We're at the edge of a
black hole so my life is just

00:09:42.738 --> 00:09:44.138
- Since 1872 that
show's been off the air

00:09:44.138 --> 00:09:45.678
I just want you to know.

00:09:45.678 --> 00:09:47.438
- So I would say the idea
that holograms are frozen

00:09:47.438 --> 00:09:48.708
is just a technology problem.

00:09:48.708 --> 00:09:50.508
You certainly could
make a hologram

00:09:50.508 --> 00:09:52.778
that was updating the
information on the surface

00:09:52.778 --> 00:09:54.078
and therefore moving in time

00:09:54.078 --> 00:09:56.618
so it doesn't have to be
frozen to be a hologram

00:09:56.618 --> 00:09:58.458
but it does have to
be two-dimensional

00:09:58.458 --> 00:10:01.988
and nonetheless appear
three-dimensional.

00:10:01.988 --> 00:10:04.728
- Why can't we be a
three-dimensional hologram

00:10:04.728 --> 00:10:06.228
of a four-dimensional
space? That's good, huh?

00:10:06.228 --> 00:10:07.058
- Um, well, that's not bad--

00:10:07.058 --> 00:10:07.898
- That was very good.

00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:08.698
- Not bad, Neil.

00:10:09.898 --> 00:10:11.328
- If a hologram is
one dimension less.

00:10:11.328 --> 00:10:12.668
- Yes, it could be the case

00:10:12.668 --> 00:10:13.998
that there's definitely--

00:10:13.998 --> 00:10:14.838
- And are we four dimensions?

00:10:14.838 --> 00:10:16.038
- Well because

00:10:16.038 --> 00:10:17.008
- Three spaces in one time?

00:10:17.008 --> 00:10:18.208
- Yeah, so there...

00:10:18.208 --> 00:10:19.438
- [Neil] Don't go back
on us on that one.

00:10:19.438 --> 00:10:20.938
- I'm not goin' back
on you on that one

00:10:20.938 --> 00:10:22.878
but there is some confusion
about that time dimension

00:10:22.878 --> 00:10:24.348
but you know,

00:10:24.348 --> 00:10:25.348
- [Neil] Whether it behaves
as a space dimension.

00:10:25.348 --> 00:10:27.118
- It clearly does to some extent

00:10:27.118 --> 00:10:28.288
- Whether it is a
full-blooded space dimension.

00:10:28.288 --> 00:10:29.518
- But to some extent it doesn't

00:10:29.518 --> 00:10:30.248
because I can turn
left and right

00:10:30.248 --> 00:10:31.088
- in space

00:10:31.088 --> 00:10:32.358
- but I can't, in space,

00:10:32.358 --> 00:10:33.418
- I don't think my
car has that setting

00:10:33.418 --> 00:10:35.758
(Janna and Neil laughing)

00:10:35.758 --> 00:10:38.728
but I can't go forward and
backward in time, right?

00:10:38.728 --> 00:10:40.398
So I can't do both
directions in time.

00:10:40.398 --> 00:10:41.228
- You have mobility
in space coordinates

00:10:41.228 --> 00:10:42.068
and not in your time.

00:10:43.798 --> 00:10:45.298
- And I can see things
in both directions.

00:10:45.298 --> 00:10:47.038
I can see something to the
right, and I can see something

00:10:47.038 --> 00:10:48.468
to the left.

00:10:48.468 --> 00:10:50.508
I can see something in the
past, whereas all light travels

00:10:50.508 --> 00:10:51.908
to the universe and gets to me,

00:10:51.908 --> 00:10:53.438
but I can not see
something from the future.

00:10:53.438 --> 00:10:57.778
- So we are prisoners
of the present,

00:10:57.778 --> 00:11:02.788
forever trapped between
a past we cannot access

00:11:03.618 --> 00:11:04.348
and a future we cannot see.

00:11:04.348 --> 00:11:05.288
(Janna laughs)

00:11:05.288 --> 00:11:06.718
- Neil, you're such a poet.

00:11:06.718 --> 00:11:07.988
- I think that's the new
opening to the Twilight Zone.

00:11:07.988 --> 00:11:08.988
(all laughing)

00:11:08.988 --> 00:11:10.058
- [Janna] Cue the music.

00:11:10.058 --> 00:11:13.798
- Okay so that's cool, so if we,

00:11:13.798 --> 00:11:16.128
so let's get back
to the question,

00:11:16.128 --> 00:11:17.328
if we know.

00:11:17.328 --> 00:11:18.698
- [Janna] This
one question could

00:11:18.698 --> 00:11:20.238
keep us busy the whole time.

00:11:20.238 --> 00:11:22.938
- A hundred percent, if we
are a hundred percent sure

00:11:22.938 --> 00:11:24.268
that we are a hologram,

00:11:24.268 --> 00:11:26.008
should we behave
any differently?

00:11:26.008 --> 00:11:28.478
And who are we a hologram to?

00:11:28.478 --> 00:11:29.708
- Yeah so this is--

00:11:29.708 --> 00:11:31.618
- On whose black
hole surface are we?

00:11:31.618 --> 00:11:33.878
- Right so then well so
people have a harder time

00:11:33.878 --> 00:11:35.748
understanding holography
in the cosmological context

00:11:35.748 --> 00:11:37.588
moving away from the black hole

00:11:37.588 --> 00:11:39.588
but the idea is that a
black hole is the terrain

00:11:39.588 --> 00:11:41.158
on which we learn
fundamental thinking,

00:11:41.158 --> 00:11:45.098
so we should use the lessons
that we can only learn there,

00:11:45.098 --> 00:11:47.258
the evidence that
only exists there,

00:11:47.258 --> 00:11:48.928
to understand the
whole universe.

00:11:48.928 --> 00:11:50.498
- As a cue, as proxy.

00:11:50.498 --> 00:11:52.138
- Right so then we turn
around and we say oh,

00:11:52.138 --> 00:11:53.568
the universe is
expanding so rapidly

00:11:53.568 --> 00:11:55.368
we have a cosmic horizon.

00:11:55.368 --> 00:11:56.908
- [Neil] We do.

00:11:56.908 --> 00:11:58.108
- We do, there is a region
beyond which we cannot see

00:11:58.108 --> 00:12:00.108
as long as the universe
expands at this pace

00:12:00.108 --> 00:12:02.808
and the light can never get
to us, race though it might,

00:12:02.808 --> 00:12:05.618
'cause the expansion of the
universe will exceed it.

00:12:05.618 --> 00:12:08.848
- So we are analogous
cosmologically to a black hole.

00:12:08.848 --> 00:12:10.218
We're inside it's event horizon.

00:12:10.218 --> 00:12:11.988
- Yeah it's like an inverted
one and we're inside

00:12:11.988 --> 00:12:13.918
the event horizon, and
there's stuff beyond it

00:12:13.918 --> 00:12:15.158
that will never know about us

00:12:15.158 --> 00:12:16.788
and we'll never know about them

00:12:16.788 --> 00:12:19.698
and so can we make
a holography of that

00:12:19.698 --> 00:12:23.698
and then yes, can I make a
holography of just you and I?

00:12:23.698 --> 00:12:25.998
But you see we have so
much less information

00:12:25.998 --> 00:12:27.968
than the maximum we
could pack on us,

00:12:27.968 --> 00:12:29.738
that that's why
we haven't tested

00:12:29.738 --> 00:12:31.538
whether or not we're
holographic animals.

00:12:31.538 --> 00:12:35.178
You see, holography requires
a more sophisticated thing

00:12:35.178 --> 00:12:37.008
than just, like printed
words in a book,

00:12:37.008 --> 00:12:38.348
which is not holographic,

00:12:38.348 --> 00:12:40.278
but it's way less than
the maximum amount

00:12:40.278 --> 00:12:42.248
of information
that you can pack.

00:12:42.248 --> 00:12:45.458
And the hologram is an
example of packing a lot more,

00:12:45.458 --> 00:12:46.758
right?

00:12:46.758 --> 00:12:47.988
So if you made a human
being that was packing

00:12:47.988 --> 00:12:49.428
the maximum amount
of information

00:12:49.428 --> 00:12:50.658
that they possibly could,

00:12:50.658 --> 00:12:53.028
they would turn into
a black hole. (laughs)

00:12:53.028 --> 00:12:54.558
- [Harrison] Or they'd have
a really full backpack.

00:12:54.558 --> 00:12:56.568
- Yeah, so holography
doesn't say

00:12:56.568 --> 00:12:58.468
you can't have things
with less information

00:12:58.468 --> 00:13:00.738
that are just not, you
know, as extraordinary.

00:13:00.738 --> 00:13:04.168
It just says that's the
maximum that we can have

00:13:04.168 --> 00:13:07.038
and so it's all an illusion,
we really are two-dimensional.

00:13:07.038 --> 00:13:08.508
- You're flippin' me
out a little bit here,

00:13:08.508 --> 00:13:09.608
so let me just say,

00:13:09.608 --> 00:13:11.548
and I'm an astrophysicist right?

00:13:11.548 --> 00:13:12.818
- Right.

00:13:12.818 --> 00:13:15.688
- So why did you all of a sudden

00:13:15.688 --> 00:13:18.218
without anybody's permission
in this conversation

00:13:18.218 --> 00:13:22.158
equate matter with information?

00:13:22.158 --> 00:13:23.888
'Cause when I think of matter,

00:13:23.888 --> 00:13:26.328
the concentration of
matter, as a star guy,

00:13:26.328 --> 00:13:29.298
I study stars, you
pack in the matter,

00:13:29.298 --> 00:13:30.668
and you get a black hole.

00:13:30.668 --> 00:13:32.968
Now you're saying you're
gonna pack information.

00:13:32.968 --> 00:13:34.838
You get a information
black hole.

00:13:34.838 --> 00:13:36.468
I mean is that the same thing?

00:13:36.468 --> 00:13:39.408
- I think that the
shift that I'm feeling,

00:13:39.408 --> 00:13:41.638
and that I'm learning
from other people as well

00:13:41.638 --> 00:13:44.748
is that matter is
just information.

00:13:44.748 --> 00:13:46.118
So what does it mean to say,

00:13:46.118 --> 00:13:47.348
an electron is matter?

00:13:47.348 --> 00:13:49.088
What we mean is it
has a certain charge,

00:13:49.088 --> 00:13:50.518
it has a certain span,
- property.

00:13:50.518 --> 00:13:52.058
- It has a certain number
of quantum properties,

00:13:52.058 --> 00:13:53.258
and those quantum properties

00:13:53.258 --> 00:13:55.018
qualify as information.

00:13:55.018 --> 00:13:57.188
- And it completely
defines what the thing is.

00:13:57.188 --> 00:13:58.628
- And it completely, there's
no such thing as an electron

00:13:58.628 --> 00:14:00.098
that's a little bit heavier,
or a little bit lighter,

00:14:00.098 --> 00:14:01.598
spins a little faster
or a little slower,

00:14:01.598 --> 00:14:02.728
has a little more charge
or a little less charge,

00:14:02.728 --> 00:14:04.668
there's no such thing.

00:14:04.668 --> 00:14:06.538
- Every electron is identical
to every other electron.

00:14:06.538 --> 00:14:07.768
- Yeah, identical.

00:14:07.768 --> 00:14:08.708
There's literally
no history to it

00:14:08.708 --> 00:14:10.238
- It's like all the Kardashians.

00:14:10.238 --> 00:14:11.368
(all laughing)

00:14:11.368 --> 00:14:12.608
- Which one is that one?

00:14:12.608 --> 00:14:14.378
- Yeah the only thing
that I need to know

00:14:14.378 --> 00:14:15.378
is its position in space.

00:14:15.378 --> 00:14:16.378
(Neil laughing)

00:14:16.378 --> 00:14:17.808
- Exactly.

00:14:17.808 --> 00:14:19.048
- So the idea there is
that the electron is simply

00:14:19.048 --> 00:14:19.178
the collection of
information about it

00:14:23.618 --> 00:14:25.288
and that is its identity

00:14:25.288 --> 00:14:27.258
and so then we start to think,
well what else does it mean

00:14:27.258 --> 00:14:28.858
to be physically real is

00:14:28.858 --> 00:14:31.058
just that list of
quantum properties.

00:14:31.058 --> 00:14:32.258
- [Neil] Wow.

00:14:32.258 --> 00:14:33.658
- So I think that
the shift is to say,

00:14:33.658 --> 00:14:35.598
look it's just math
and information.

00:14:35.598 --> 00:14:38.438
And we gotta stop thinkin' so
concrete you know, feelin'.

00:14:38.438 --> 00:14:39.668
- Like physically.

00:14:39.668 --> 00:14:40.898
- Like, we know that
this is an illusion.

00:14:40.898 --> 00:14:42.138
I can't put my hand
through your leg

00:14:42.138 --> 00:14:43.408
even though we both know

00:14:43.408 --> 00:14:44.568
it's mostly empty
space, both parts.

00:14:44.568 --> 00:14:46.738
- We are mostly
empty frickin' space.

00:14:46.738 --> 00:14:48.408
- Yeah, voids, two voids.

00:14:48.408 --> 00:14:49.848
- That's the secret
to one of my tricks,

00:14:49.848 --> 00:14:51.248
I won't tell you which one.

00:14:51.248 --> 00:14:53.278
- 'Cause you're also
a magician, right.

00:14:53.278 --> 00:14:55.448
- [Harrison] It's all quantum.

00:14:55.448 --> 00:14:57.848
- There's a famous comment,
I'm gonna paraphrase it

00:14:57.848 --> 00:15:00.388
and it might be apocryphal
but it's great anyway,

00:15:00.388 --> 00:15:02.958
Rutherford, was it
Rutherford who discovered

00:15:02.958 --> 00:15:04.428
that the atom was empty?

00:15:04.428 --> 00:15:05.658
I think it was Rutherford.

00:15:05.658 --> 00:15:06.958
- Yeah I mean wasn't he
doing the nuclear stuff?

00:15:06.958 --> 00:15:08.468
- He did the gold
foil experiments.

00:15:08.468 --> 00:15:10.468
So what you do is you get
a really thin gold foil,

00:15:10.468 --> 00:15:13.138
like aluminum foil,
this is gold foil,

00:15:13.138 --> 00:15:14.638
hammer it really thin.

00:15:14.638 --> 00:15:16.708
- That's what I've seen Trump
wraps all his sandwiches in.

00:15:16.708 --> 00:15:17.708
- I was gonna say, aah!

00:15:17.708 --> 00:15:19.908
- Trump gilded everything.

00:15:19.908 --> 00:15:24.778
So then you hang it there
and you fire particles at it

00:15:24.778 --> 00:15:28.688
and you ask, how often does
a particle hit a gold atom

00:15:28.688 --> 00:15:30.118
and how often does
it pass through?

00:15:30.118 --> 00:15:31.858
'Cause you make it
really, really thin,

00:15:31.858 --> 00:15:35.858
and it turns out most particles
pass right through the foil.

00:15:35.858 --> 00:15:37.088
- [Janna] No damage done.

00:15:37.088 --> 00:15:38.628
- No damage done, not
deflected, nothing.

00:15:38.628 --> 00:15:42.228
And he concluded
that most of atoms

00:15:42.228 --> 00:15:45.568
are completely empty
space, and he's rumored,

00:15:45.568 --> 00:15:47.668
the next morning, after
having contemplated this,

00:15:47.668 --> 00:15:51.878
that he was afraid to
step onto the floor

00:15:51.878 --> 00:15:53.578
getting out of his bed

00:15:53.578 --> 00:15:54.778
- For fear of falling in?

00:15:54.778 --> 00:15:56.348
- For fear he might
fall through the floor.

00:15:56.348 --> 00:16:01.318
'Cause he alone on earth knew
how empty matter actually is.

00:16:02.718 --> 00:16:04.488
- I figured that out by
looking at Republican heads.

00:16:04.488 --> 00:16:05.588
(all laughing)

00:16:05.588 --> 00:16:06.658
They're very empty.

00:16:06.658 --> 00:16:08.258
- Reading their Tweets.

00:16:08.258 --> 00:16:09.888
- Yeah exactly.

00:16:09.888 --> 00:16:11.998
- So in astrophysics
there's something called

00:16:11.998 --> 00:16:14.258
a degenerate state,
which is not a statement

00:16:14.258 --> 00:16:16.098
about its moral fiber, but--

00:16:16.098 --> 00:16:17.328
- I was gonna say most comedians

00:16:17.328 --> 00:16:18.868
are in a degenerate state.

00:16:18.868 --> 00:16:21.908
- Degenerate state has to
do with whether two atoms

00:16:21.908 --> 00:16:25.278
or particles can occupy the
same state at the same time,

00:16:25.278 --> 00:16:26.878
the same configuration.

00:16:26.878 --> 00:16:30.148
And electrons, two
identical electrons

00:16:30.148 --> 00:16:31.948
cannot be in the same
place at the same time,

00:16:31.948 --> 00:16:33.418
they have to be separated.

00:16:33.418 --> 00:16:37.218
And so there's a pressure
because they will not,

00:16:37.218 --> 00:16:39.418
there's a limit to how
close you can get them.

00:16:39.418 --> 00:16:41.458
And now based on
how you describe it

00:16:41.458 --> 00:16:44.388
I'm thinking if you do that

00:16:44.388 --> 00:16:46.058
you're putting too much
information in one place.

00:16:46.058 --> 00:16:48.228
- Ooh that's an interesting
idea, look at you Neil.

00:16:48.228 --> 00:16:51.498
So the Pauli exclusion
principle would be caused by

00:16:51.498 --> 00:16:53.368
- An information--

00:16:53.368 --> 00:16:54.898
- An information limit.

00:16:54.898 --> 00:16:56.368
- Wanna write a paper on that?

00:16:56.368 --> 00:16:57.238
- Let's do it, oh my God, I'm
gonna be up all night now.

00:16:57.238 --> 00:16:58.238
- Let's do that.

00:16:58.238 --> 00:16:59.708
- Ah, I love it.

00:16:59.708 --> 00:17:02.108
- All of the degenerate states,
it's an information problem.

00:17:02.108 --> 00:17:03.248
- I love it I think
that's a lovely idea.

00:17:03.248 --> 00:17:04.378
We'll talk about that later.

00:17:04.378 --> 00:17:05.208
- [Neil] We gotta take a break.

00:17:05.208 --> 00:17:06.648
- Do I get co-credit?

00:17:06.648 --> 00:17:07.918
(Neil and Janna laughing)

00:17:07.918 --> 00:17:09.548
am I gonna be in a paper?

00:17:09.548 --> 00:17:11.988
- Yeah the credits,
yeah Harrison made
us laugh during this.

00:17:11.988 --> 00:17:13.218
During these paragraphs.

00:17:13.218 --> 00:17:14.818
- Moral support, I
was the cheerleader.

00:17:14.818 --> 00:17:16.058
- So when we come back,

