00:45:04.704 --> 00:45:06.434
So the expansion
of the universe,

00:45:06.434 --> 00:45:08.604
is think of it as
a stretchy fabric

00:45:08.604 --> 00:45:11.674
that if, and it doesn't
matter where you are,

00:45:11.674 --> 00:45:13.914
you'll think you're at the
center of that stretching

00:45:13.914 --> 00:45:16.014
and you'll see objects
moving away from you

00:45:16.014 --> 00:45:17.484
in every direction.

00:45:17.484 --> 00:45:20.854
Objects that are near you will
not be moving away as fast

00:45:20.854 --> 00:45:23.224
as objects that
are farther away.

00:45:23.224 --> 00:45:27.064
'Cause every, let's say for
every meter it stretches

00:45:27.064 --> 00:45:28.664
ten centimeters, okay?

00:45:28.664 --> 00:45:31.564
Well that means at two meters
it stretches 20 centimeters.

00:45:31.564 --> 00:45:33.564
At three meters
it's 30 centimeters.

00:45:33.564 --> 00:45:35.364
That's how stretching
works in the fabric.

00:45:35.364 --> 00:45:40.344
So, all galaxies have speeds
relative to each other.

00:45:41.504 --> 00:45:46.274
If you have a nearby
galaxy it's goin' faster

00:45:47.484 --> 00:45:50.954
than the stretching
speed of the universe

00:45:50.954 --> 00:45:53.524
in the space near me,

00:45:53.524 --> 00:45:57.654
so my gravity overcomes
anything the universe is doing

00:45:57.654 --> 00:45:59.124
for things nearby.

00:45:59.124 --> 00:46:01.694
So nearby galaxies are
goin' on like the universe

00:46:01.694 --> 00:46:03.464
is not expanding, they
don't really care.

00:46:03.464 --> 00:46:05.764
Their gravity is strong
enough to create motions

00:46:05.764 --> 00:46:09.734
that overcome the expansion
of the universe itself.

00:46:09.734 --> 00:46:12.204
But we're not gonna collide
with a distant galaxy

00:46:12.204 --> 00:46:13.874
'cause the stretching
of the universe

00:46:13.874 --> 00:46:16.644
is taking us away faster
than that can ever happen.

00:46:16.644 --> 00:46:17.844
- I just wanted to
make reference to,

00:46:17.844 --> 00:46:20.684
